WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Multi-head attention.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.961
A single attention map has to serve every purpose at once.

3
00:00:05.961 --> 00:00:07.067
So we run several.

4
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:12.500
Multi-head attention runs h attention operations in parallel.

5
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:20.233
Each head has its own learned query, key, and value projections, working in a smaller subspace of size d over h.

6
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.100
We concatenate the results and mix them with an output matrix.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.467
Here is the picture.

8
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:29.300
The token vectors fan out into four heads.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.967
Each head, with private projections, runs its own attention.

10
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:34.867
They all work at the same time.

11
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.700
Then concatenation stitches their findings back into one vector per token.

12
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:41.560
Why bother?

13
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.300
Heads are free to specialize.

14
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.000
In trained models, researchers find heads tracking syntax.

15
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.167
Heads that resolve coreference.

16
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:51.600
Heads that copy adjacent tokens.

17
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.900
And heads that look up rare tokens.

18
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.700
And it's free in compute terms.

19
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.267
Eight heads of sixty four dimensions add up to five twelve, the same as one full-width head.

20
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.300
In code, it's a tensor shuffle.

21
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.600
Six tokens, sixty four dimensions, four heads.

22
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.200
One reshape and transpose splits the vectors into heads, with no loops.

23
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.042
We compute one attention map per head, then concatenate.

24
00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:18.433
Four maps, one output.

25
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:21.167
Here's a debugging story.

26
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:24.067
Your reranker demotes a clearly relevant chunk.

27
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.136
You dump its attention maps.

28
00:01:26.136 --> 00:01:34.667
Head seven, which usually links query terms to matches, never fires on EVA, because the chunk spells it out as extravehicular activity.

29
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:40.633
Per head maps turn the model is being weird into a fix: expand abbreviations at indexing.

30
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:42.633
Let's recap.

31
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.700
Multi-head attention runs h heads in parallel.

32
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.200
Each has its own query, key, and value projections.

33
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.033
Outputs are concatenated and mixed by an output matrix.

34
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:56.519
The cost is the same, but the patterns are richer.

35
00:01:56.519 --> 00:01:58.367
Next up: positional encoding.
